## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement a compact ranking structure with duplicates and a fixed value range, using O(log U) updates and O(log U) kth-order queries.

## Key Concepts

- Fenwick tree for prefix sums
- Binary lifting to find k-th order statistic (standard BIT trick: find largest power of 2 <= n, then iterate downward checking cumulative tree values)
- Handling duplicates via per-value counts

## Code Example

```javascript
class Fenwick {
  constructor(n) { 
    this.n = n; 
    this.tree = new Array(n + 1).fill(0); 
  }

  add(i, delta) {
    for (; i <= this.n; i += i & -i) this.tree[i] += delta;
  }

  sum(i) {
    let s = 0;
    for (; i > 0; i -= i & -i) s += this.tree[i];
    return s;
  }

  kth(k) {
    if (k < 1) return -1;
    const total = this.sum(this.n);
    if (k > total) return -1;

    let idx = 0;
    let bit = 1;
    while (bit << 1 <= this.n) bit <<= 1;

    let sum = 0;
    for (; bit > 0; bit >>= 1) {
      const next = idx + bit;
      if (next <= this.n && sum + this.tree[next] < k) {
        sum += this.tree[next];
        idx = next;
      }
    }
    return idx; // 0-indexed
  }
}

class Leaderboard {
  constructor(maxScore = 1000000) {
    this.bit = new Fenwick(maxScore + 1);
  }

  insertScore(s) { this.bit.add(s + 1, 1); }

  eraseScore(s) { this.bit.add(s + 1, -1); }

  getKthScore(k) { return this.bit.kth(k) - 1; }

  getRank(s) { return this.bit.sum(s + 1); }
}
```

## Handling Ties

Since each score has its own count, duplicates naturally work:
- insertScore(50) adds count at index 50
- insertScore(50) again increments same index
- eraseScore(50) decrements it
- getRank(50) returns all scores <= 50 including duplicates
- getKthScore(k) skips to the k-th element accounting for counts

## Why This Is Asked

Dynamic string editing with fast substring queries is critical in modern editors and versioned docs. A rope with an implicit treap provides randomized balance and localized updates, enabling logarithmic edits while maintaining per-substring hashes for quick comparisons.

## Key Concepts

- Implicit treap (rope) structure for sequence storage
- Subtree size and rolling hash maintenance
- Polynomial rolling hash with two moduli to reduce collisions
- Split and merge operations for positional edits
- Substring hash isolation without copying strings

## Code Example

```javascript
class Node {
  constructor(ch) {
    this.c = ch;
    this.pr = Math.random();
    this.left = null; this.right = null;
    this.sz = 1;
    this.h1 = ch.charCodeAt(0);
    this.h2 = ch.charCodeAt(0);
  }
}
// Update size and hashes from children
function upd(n){ if(!n) return; n.sz = 1; n.h1 = n.c.charCodeAt(0); n.h2 = n.c.charCodeAt(0);
  if(n.left){ n.sz += n.left.sz; n.h1 = n.left.h1 + somePow1; n.h2 = n.left.h2 + somePow2; }
  if(n.right){ n.sz += n.right.sz; n.h1 = n.h1 + rightHash1; n.h2 = n.h2 + rightHash2; }
}
function split(t, k){ // split first k chars to a
  if(!t) return [null, null];
  if(size(t.left) >= k){ const [l, r] = split(t.left, k); t.left = r; upd(t); return [l, t]; }
  const [l, r] = split(t.right, k - size(t.left) - 1); t.right = l; upd(t); return [t, r];
}
function merge(a, b){ if(!a||!b) return a||b; if(a.pr < b.pr){ a.right = merge(a.right, b); upd(a); return a; } else { b.left = merge(a, b.left); upd(b); return b; } }
```

