## Why This Question Is Asked
This question tests understanding of stack augmentation, a practical pattern for maintaining auxiliary state. It demonstrates the ability to design data structures that support efficient queries while handling edge cases like duplicates and empty states.

## Key Concepts
- Two auxiliary stacks (minStack and maxStack) track running minima and maxima in sync with the main stack
- Each push/pop operation updates all three stacks atomically, preserving O(1) time complexity
- Duplicates are handled naturally since the min/max is recalculated at each step
- Empty stack edge cases must be handled (typically by checking stack length before accessing top)

## Code Example
```javascript
class MinMaxStack {
  constructor() {
    this.stack = [];
    this.minStack = [];
    this.maxStack = [];
  }

  push(x) {
    this.stack.push(x);
    this.minStack.push(
      this.minStack.length ? Math.min(x, this.minStack[this.minStack.length - 1]) : x
    );
    this.maxStack.push(
      this.maxStack.length ? Math.max(x, this.maxStack[this.maxStack.length - 1]) : x
    );
  }

  pop() {
    if (this.stack.length === 0) return undefined;
    this.minStack.pop();
    this.maxStack.pop();
    return this.stack.pop();
  }

  top() {
    return this.stack[this.stack.length - 1];
  }

  getMin() {
    return this.minStack[this.minStack.length - 1];
  }

  getMax() {
    return this.maxStack[this.maxStack.length - 1];
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Evaluates ability to implement streaming statistics with efficient data structures and real-time median calculation.

## Key Concepts

- Dual heap structure (max-heap + min-heap)
- Balance invariant maintenance
- Median computation in O(log n) per insert
- Streaming data processing

## Code Example

```javascript
class MedianStream {
  constructor() {
    this.low = new MaxHeap();  // Lower half
    this.high = new MinHeap(); // Upper half
  }

  insert(x) {
    // Insert into appropriate heap and rebalance
    if (this.low.isEmpty() || x <= this.low.peek()) {
      this.low.push(x);
    } else {
      this.high.push(x);
    }

    // Rebalance to maintain size difference ≤ 1
    if (this.low.size() > this.high.size() + 1) {
      this.high.push(this.low.pop());
    } else if (this.high.size() > this.low.size() + 1) {
      this.low.push(this.high.pop());
    }
  }

  getMedian() {
    if (this.low.size() > this.high.size()) {
      return this.low.peek();
    } else if (this.high.size() > this.low.size()) {
      return this.high.peek();
    } else {
      return (this.low.peek() + this.high.peek()) / 2;
    }
  }
}
```

## Implementation Notes

- Duplicates are handled naturally by heap properties
- Memory usage is O(n) for storing all elements
- Suitable for real-time streaming applications
- Can be extended to support removal operations with additional bookkeeping

