## Why This Is Asked
Tests designing a mutable priority structure with time-based expiry and top-k queries, including updates and duplicates, under realistic constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Lazy expiry in priority queues to avoid frequent rebalancing
- O(log n) updates via delete+insert using an id map
- Tie-breaking with a unique id sequence to handle duplicates
- Top-k retrieval by temporarily extracting and reinserting items

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node {
  constructor(id, score, expiry, seq) {
    this.id = id; this.score = score; this.expiry = expiry; this.seq = seq;
  }
}
class DynamicPriorityQueue {
  constructor() {
    this.treap = new Treap();
    this.idMap = new Map();
    this.seqCounter = 0;
  }

  insertItem(id, score, expiry) {
    this.removeItem(id);
    const node = new Node(id, score, expiry, ++this.seqCounter);
    this.treap.insert(node);
    this.idMap.set(id, node);
  }

  updateScore(id, newScore) {
    const node = this.idMap.get(id);
    if (node) {
      this.insertItem(id, newScore, node.expiry);
    }
  }

  removeItem(id) {
    const node = this.idMap.get(id);
    if (node) {
      this.treap.remove(node);
      this.idMap.delete(id);
    }
  }

  queryTopK(now, k) {
    const result = [];
    const iterator = this.treap.inOrder();
    for (const node of iterator) {
      if (result.length >= k) break;
      if (node.expiry > now) {
        result.push({id: node.id, score: node.score});
      }
    }
    return result;
  }
}
```

## Memory Trade-offs
- Additional O(n) space for id map and sequence counters
- Expired items remain in memory until explicitly removed or overwritten
- Lazy expiry reduces computational overhead at the cost of memory usage

